<UserControl
    x:Class="ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Player.MiniPlayerChromeView"
    xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:automation="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Automation;assembly=Avalonia.Controls"
    xmlns:shell="clr-namespace:ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Shell"
    x:DataType="shell:ShellViewModel"
    automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource MiniPlayerChromeAccessibleName}">
  <!--
    The prototype's composition, which until 2026-08-28 was five buttons and nothing else: a three
    pixel bar of progress across the top, then the title and the clock on the left with the five on
    the right. What it fixes is not decoration - a mini player showed neither what was playing nor
    how far in it was, which is most of what a picture-in-picture window is for.
  -->
  <!--
    The band paints the player's surface and not the shell's, and that is a correction rather than a
    style. The five wear player-chrome, which sets PlayerTextBrush (#F8FAFC), and this panel used to
    paint ShellSurfaceBrush - #FBFCFE in the light theme. Measured before the change: 1.02:1, five
    buttons with nothing visible on them. No contrast gate saw it because every one of them reads the
    four dictionaries, and a dictionary is consistent with itself; what was wrong was the pairing,
    which exists only in this view. MiniPlayerBandTests measures the pairing.
  -->
  <DockPanel
      x:Name="MiniPlayerChromeBand"
      Background="{DynamicResource PlayerSurfaceBrush}">
    <!--
      The track stays and only the fill comes and goes, which keeps the band exactly as tall from
      the first frame to the last. The window answers a drag by putting 16:9 back on the picture and
      adding this band's height on top - a bar that appeared the moment the engine reported a
      duration would change that height under a window nobody was resizing.

      Three setters and not one: the base theme gives a ProgressBar a minimum height of four and a
      minimum width of 200, measured in the library card and again in the episode row, so Height
      alone draws four and stretching inside 320 px of window clips rather than fits.
    -->
    <Panel
        DockPanel.Dock="Top"
        Height="3"
        Background="{DynamicResource PlayerHairlineBrush}">
      <!--
        Absent while the length is unknown, for the reason DurationSeconds is written the way it is:
        it answers 1 until the engine says otherwise, so a position of two minutes would be clamped
        to the maximum and paint a full bar over a film that has just started.
      -->
      <ProgressBar
          x:Name="MiniPlayerProgress"
          Height="3"
          MinHeight="3"
          MinWidth="0"
          Background="Transparent"
          Foreground="{DynamicResource AccentBrush}"
          IsVisible="{Binding Player.Player.Transport.HasDuration}"
          Minimum="0"
          Maximum="{Binding Player.Player.Transport.DurationSeconds}"
          Value="{Binding Player.Player.Transport.PositionSeconds}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportPositionLabel}" />
    </Panel>
    <DockPanel Margin="12,10">
      <!--
        A WrapPanel and not a row: the window's own minimum is 320, and the five carried translated
        words until 2026-08-21 - measured folding into three rows inside 480x270, which is what the
        glyphs of §4 were for. The wrapping stays, because a glyph shrinks the row without proving it
        can never wrap: this is the shape that has put a control outside the window six times in this
        repository.

        Docked right rather than centred since the readout arrived beside it: the two share one line,
        and a centred panel would take the middle of what is left and leave the title against the
        edge.
      -->
      <!--
        Only Content changed here. AutomationProperties.Name still points at the resource key, so the
        five answer to the same names the walk presses and a screen reader still reads a word rather
        than a private-use codepoint. The font list lives in the player-chrome class, in one place
        for all eleven.
      -->
      <!--
        The spacing is the panel's and not the buttons'. It lived in the player-chrome class as a
        Margin until the large transport adopted that class and would have inherited a separation it
        did not want.
      -->
      <WrapPanel
          x:Name="MiniPlayerChromeSurface"
          DockPanel.Dock="Right"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          ItemSpacing="{DynamicResource Space8}"
          LineSpacing="{DynamicResource Space8}">
        <!--
          Back, play, forward, expand, close - the prototype's order, and taken here where the large
          transport could not take it. There the three of the session and the two of the skips come
          from models built at different moments, so interleaving them means moving commands between
          them; these five sit in one panel and read one view model, so the order is the markup's.
        -->
        <Button
            x:Name="MiniPlayerSkipBack"
            Classes="player-chrome"
            Command="{Binding Player.Player.Transport.SkipBackwardCommand}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource MiniPlayerSkipBack}">
          <Path Classes="icon size-20" Data="{DynamicResource IconSkipBackward}" />
        </Button>
        <Button
            x:Name="MiniPlayerPlayPause"
            Classes="player-chrome"
            Command="{Binding Player.Player.TogglePlaybackCommand}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource MiniPlayerPlayPause}">
          <Path Classes="icon size-22 filled" Data="{DynamicResource IconPlay}" />
        </Button>
        <Button
            x:Name="MiniPlayerSkipForward"
            Classes="player-chrome"
            Command="{Binding Player.Player.Transport.SkipForwardCommand}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource MiniPlayerSkipForward}">
          <Path Classes="icon size-20" Data="{DynamicResource IconSkipForward}" />
        </Button>
        <Button
            x:Name="MiniPlayerRestore"
            Classes="player-chrome"
            Command="{Binding ToggleMiniPlayerCommand}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource MiniPlayerRestore}">
          <Path Classes="icon size-20" Data="{DynamicResource IconExitFullscreen}" />
        </Button>
        <Button
            x:Name="MiniPlayerClose"
            Classes="player-chrome"
            Command="{Binding ClosePlayerCommand}"
            automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource MiniPlayerClose}">
          <Path Classes="icon size-20" Data="{DynamicResource IconClose}" />
        </Button>
      </WrapPanel>
      <!--
        What is playing and where it is, which the window did not say at all. Both lines are one line
        each and trimmed rather than wrapped: at 320 px the five buttons take 252 of the 296 the band
        has, so whatever is left is a strip, and a strip is for an ellipsis and not for a second row
        of a title.

        The clock is one string from the model rather than three bound side by side, because the
        separators between them are punctuation the prototype draws and not words a dictionary holds
        - and three TextBlocks in a row is the shape that folds when the middle one is empty.
      -->
      <StackPanel x:Name="MiniPlayerReadout" Margin="0,0,8,0" VerticalAlignment="Center">
        <TextBlock
            x:Name="MiniPlayerTitleText"
            FontSize="{DynamicResource FontSizeCaption}"
            FontWeight="SemiBold"
            Foreground="{DynamicResource PlayerTextBrush}"
            IsVisible="{Binding HasPlayerTitle}"
            Text="{Binding PlayerTitle}"
            TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
            TextWrapping="NoWrap" />
        <TextBlock
            x:Name="MiniPlayerClockText"
            FontSize="{DynamicResource FontSizeCaption}"
            Foreground="{DynamicResource PlayerTextSecondaryBrush}"
            Text="{Binding Player.Player.Transport.Readout}"
            TextTrimming="CharacterEllipsis"
            TextWrapping="NoWrap" />
      </StackPanel>
    </DockPanel>
  </DockPanel>
</UserControl>
